Crash when entry point parameter is a struct containing an interface-typed field
## Issue Description

Compiling an entry point whose `uniform` parameter is a struct that contains an interface-typed field causes a segfault on SPIRV and an internal compiler error (99999) on HLSL. The crash occurs even when type conformances are registered via `-conformance` flags.

## Reproducer Code

```hlsl
[anyValueSize(16)]
interface IFoo
{
    float getValue();
}

struct ImplA : IFoo
{
    float v;
    float getValue() { return v; }
}

struct Params
{
    IFoo foo;
    float scale;
}

RWStructuredBuffer<float> outputBuffer;

[numthreads(1, 1, 1)]
void computeMain(uniform Params p)
{
    outputBuffer[0] = p.foo.getValue() * p.scale;
}
```

**Command:**
```bash
slangc test.slang -target spirv -stage compute -entry computeMain -conformance "ImplA:IFoo=0"
```

## Expected Behavior

The compiler should either successfully compile the code (generating dispatch code for the nested interface field) or produce a clear diagnostic explaining that struct-typed entry point parameters with interface fields are not supported.

## Actual Behavior

- SPIRV: segfault (exit code 139, no error message)
- HLSL: `internal error 99999: unimplemented feature in Slang compiler: unexpected IR opcode during code emit`

Note: a direct interface-typed entry point parameter (`uniform IFoo foo`) works correctly with conformances.

## Test Plan

Enable the disabled test `diagnose-entry-point-interface-in-struct.slang` in #10260.
